import Sheaf.RingTheory.GlobalDimension
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Cotangent

/-!
# Embedding dimension bounds the length of a residue-field resolution

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 00OA
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-residue-field-pd-ge-embedding-dimension).
-/

universe u

namespace IsLocalRing

/-- In a Noetherian local ring, every projective resolution of the residue field of length
at most `d` satisfies `dim (m/m²) ≤ d`. Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 00OA
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-residue-field-pd-ge-embedding-dimension).

Proof:
1. The maximal ideal is finitely generated by `def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring`, so its
   cotangent space is finite dimensional. Choose lifts `x₁, ..., xₙ` of a residue-field
   basis of `m/m²`. If `n = 0`, the result follows from `0 ≤ d`.
2. Form the free Koszul complex `Kᵢ` on the `i`-element subsets of `{1, ..., n}`
   (`def-weil-pbc-free-module`), with differential
   `e_J ↦ ∑ₐ (-1)^(a+1) x_(jₐ) e_(J\{jₐ})`. Removing two indices in the opposite
   orders gives opposite signs, hence the differential squares to zero. Its augmentation
   to the residue field kills the first differential. All differential coefficients lie in `m`.
3. The residue field is generated by `1` (`def-weil-finitely-generated-module`). Apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-minimal-finite-free-resolution-local` to the given resolution of length
   at most `d` (`def-weil-pbc-finite-global-dimension`): obtain a finite free exact resolution
   `F` with differential images in `mF`. Exactness is `def-weil-pbc-exact-sequence`.
   Extend it by zero terms above degree `d`.
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-free-complex-maps-to-resolution`, lift the identity of the residue field
   to a chain map `α : K → F` compatible with the augmentations.
5. For a finite free module `G`, its basis gives an isomorphism
   `(G/mG)^n → mG/m²G`, sending `(yⱼ)` to the class of `∑ⱼ xⱼ yⱼ`.
   Changing lifts changes this sum only by `m²G`. The isomorphism is natural for linear
   maps of free modules. Thus injectivity modulo `m` implies injectivity on `mG/m²G`.
   A map with image in `mG'` also induces a map `G/mG → mG'/m²G'`.
6. Apply these induced maps to the chain-map identity to obtain the commutative square
   with left vertical map `αᵢ mod m`, right vertical map `α_(i-1)` on `mG/m²G`, and
   horizontal maps induced by the two differentials.
7. The induced Koszul differential is injective for `1 ≤ i ≤ n`: for each subset `J`,
   choose `j ∈ J`; the coefficient of `e_(J\{j})` in the `j`-th component is precisely
   the signed coefficient of `e_J`. Hence a vector in the kernel has all coefficients zero.
   For `i > n`, the source is zero.
8. Induct on `i` to show `αᵢ mod m` injective. In degree zero, the augmentation identity
   gives a left inverse. In higher degrees, step 5 makes the right vertical map injective;
   steps 6 and 7 then force the left vertical map to be injective.
9. The top Koszul term modulo `m` is the nonzero residue field. Its injection into
   `Fₙ/mFₙ` forces `Fₙ ≠ 0`. Since `Fᵢ = 0` for `i > d`, we conclude `n ≤ d`. -/
theorem finrank_cotangentSpace_le_of_hasProjectiveResolutionLE
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] (d : ℕ)
    (h : ModuleCat.HasProjectiveResolutionLE (ModuleCat.of R (ResidueField R)) d) :
    Module.finrank (ResidueField R) (CotangentSpace R) ≤ d := by
  sorry

end IsLocalRing
